WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.240
Today we are going to see if Waymo’s AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:15.040
can recreate a virtual copy of San&nbsp;
Francisco from 2.8 million photos.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.040
To be able to do that, they rely on a previous&nbsp;
AI-based technique that can take a collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:27.840
of photos like these, and magically, create a&nbsp;
video where we can fly through these photos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:35.040
This is what we call a NERF-based technique,&nbsp;
and these are truly amazing. Essentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:43.520
photos go in, the AI fills in the gaps, and&nbsp;
reality comes out. And there are a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.280
gaps between these photos, all of which are&nbsp;
filled in with high-quality synthetic data.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:57.520
So, as you see with these previous methods, great&nbsp;
leaps are being made, but one thing stayed more or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:05.600
less the same: and that is, the scale of these&nbsp;
scenes is not that big. So, scientists at Waymo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:13.360
had a crazy idea, and they said, rendering&nbsp;
just a tiny scene is not that useful. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:21.600
millions of photos laying around, why not render&nbsp;
an entire city like this? So, can Waymo do that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:30.480
Well, maybe, but that would take Waymo'. I am&nbsp;
sorry. I am so sorry, I just couldn't resist.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.680
Well, let’s see what they came up with. Look,&nbsp;
these self-driving cars are going around the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:45.440
they take photos along their journey, and…well,&nbsp;
I have to say that I am a little skeptical here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:52.880
Have a look at what previous techniques could do&nbsp;
with this dataset. This is not really usable, so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:59.360
could Waymo pull this off? Well, hold on to&nbsp;
your papers, and let’s have a look together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.560
My goodness…this is their fully reconstructed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.800
3D neighborhood from these&nbsp;
photos. Wow. That is superb.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.760
And don’t forget, most of this&nbsp;
information is synthetic. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:24.880
filled in by the AI. Does this mean that? Yes!&nbsp;
Yes it does! It means three amazing things.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.280
One, we can drive a different path that&nbsp;
has not been driven before by these cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.840
and still see the city correctly.&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.240
Two, we can look at these&nbsp;
buildings from viewpoints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.800
that we don’t have enough information about,&nbsp;
and the AI fills in the rest of the details.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:55.760
So cool! But it doesn't end there, not even close.
Three, here comes my favorite. We can also engage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:02.640
in what they call appearance modulation.&nbsp;
Yes, some of the driving took place at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:09.760
some during the daytime, so we have information&nbsp;
about the change of the lighting conditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:16.480
What does that mean? It means that we can&nbsp;
even fuse all this information together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:23.760
and choose the time of day for our virtual&nbsp;
city. That is absolutely amazing. I love it.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:31.280
Yes, of course, not even this technique&nbsp;
is perfect, the resolution and the details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:37.680
should definitely be improved over time.&nbsp;
Plus, it does well with a stationary city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.640
but with dynamic moving objects, not so&nbsp;
much. But do not forget, the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.680
first NERF paper was published just&nbsp;
two years ago, and it could do this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:57.840
And now, just a couple papers down the line, and&nbsp;
we have not only these tiny scenes, but entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:05.760
city blocks. So much improvement in just a couple&nbsp;
papers. How cool is that. Absolutely amazing.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.120
And with this, we can drive and play&nbsp;
around in a beautiful virtual world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:17.920
that is a copy of the real world around us. And&nbsp;
now, if we wish it to be a little different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.600
we can even have our freedom&nbsp;
in changing this world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.800
according to our artistic vision. I would&nbsp;
love to see more work in this direction.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:33.440
But wait, here comes the big question. What&nbsp;
is all this good for? Well, one of the answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:41.440
is sim2real. What is that? Sim2real means&nbsp;
training an AI in a simulated world, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.680
trying to teach it everything it needs to learn&nbsp;
there before deploying it into the real world.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:54.640
Here is an amazing example, look, OpenAI&nbsp;
trained their robot hand in a simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:02.400
to be able to rotate these Rubik cubes. And then,&nbsp;
deployed this software onto a real robot hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.640
and, look! It can use this simulation knowledge,&nbsp;
and now it works in the real world too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:17.200
But sim2real has relevance to self-driving cars&nbsp;
too. Look. Tesla is already working on creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:24.080
virtual worlds, and training their cars there. One&nbsp;
of the advantages of that is that we can create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:32.160
really unlikely and potentially unsafe scenarios,&nbsp;
but, in these virtual worlds, the self-driving AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:38.480
can train itself safely. And when they deploy&nbsp;
them into the real world, they will have all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:46.640
this knowledge. It is fantastic to see Waymo also&nbsp;
moving in this direction. What a time to be alive!

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.880
So, what would you use this for? What do you&nbsp;
expect to happen a couple more papers down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.800
line? Please let me know in the comments&nbsp;
below. I’d love to hear your thoughts.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.240
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

